	abstract = {Accurate endoscopic identification of Inflammatory Bowel Disease (IBD) subtypes is crucial for clinical management, yet existing computational methods are constrained by visual representations that lack semantic context and fail to model long-range dependencies. To overcome these limitations, we introduce Semba, a novel semantic-visual representation learning method for automated IBD subtype identification. Semba employs a dual-stream architecture that synergistically integrates semantic generation and visual validation. The workflow begins with a multimodal large language model (MLLM) that translates raw endoscopic imagery into a structured semantic representation, bridging the gap between visual patterns and clinical findings. Concurrently, a hierarchical MambaVision architecture efficiently extracts a multi-scale visual representation, capturing both local and global features with linear complexity. Finally, a bidirectional fusion mechanism dynamically aligns and integrates these two representations using cross-attention and adaptive gating, culminating in a robust, semantically-aligned feature representation for precise subtype identification. Rigorous evaluation on a large-scale clinical IBD cohort and a public benchmark (WCE) demonstrates that Semba achieves state-of-the-art accuracy of 96.49% and 99.56% on IBD-1431 and WCE datasets, respectively, outperforming the strongest visual baseline by 0.27 and 0.45 percentage points in accuracy. This work establishes a new paradigm for computational endoscopy, showcasing the potential of semantic-visual representation learning in automated endoscopic assessment. © 2026},
