	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105016521648?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Automatically generating accurate diagnostic reports from nasopharyngoscopy images holds significant clinical value. However, the lack of publicly available training datasets in this domain hinders the fine-tuning of pretrained models. Moreover, general-purpose large models often suffer from hallucinations due to the absence of empirical and structured prompt design. To address these challenges, we propose TfMC, a Training-free Modular Collaborative framework that synergistically integrates Multimodal Vision-Language Models (MVLMs) with Large Language Models (LLMs) for zero-shot nasopharyngoscopy report generation. We propose a diagnostic decision tree-driven prompt generation strategy based on clinical expertise, converting node routing decisions into visual question-answering to dynamically guide prompt generation. Additionally, we design a symptom-based image-node matching and knowledge generation strategy to enhance the evidential and targeted of prompts. Experimental results on a clinically dataset demonstrate that our framework effectively mitigates hallucination in zero-shot settings while ensure consistency between report content and diagnostic result, significantly improving report accuracy and interpretability.  © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).},
